					'The conditional doesn\'t have its own separate list of irregular stems. It reuses the exact same twelve verbs and the same three stem patterns as the simple future. Five verbs drop the infinitive\'s final vowel: *caber* → *cabr-*, *haber* → *habr-*, *poder* → *podr-*, *querer* → *querr-*, *saber* → *sabr-*. Five drop the vowel and insert a *-d-*: *poner* → *pondr-*, *salir* → *saldr-*, *tener* → *tendr-*, *valer* → *valdr-*, *venir* → *vendr-*. Two are simply irregular: *decir* → *dir-*, *hacer* → *har-*.',
					'Once the stem is set, the regular conditional endings attach exactly as they would to any other verb: *tener* becomes *tendría, tendrías, tendría, tendríamos, tendríais, tendrían*. Anyone who has already learned the twelve future-tense irregular stems gets the conditional\'s irregulars for free: it\'s the identical list, just paired with a different ending set. [Ser](/ser-conjugation/), true to form, isn\'t on the list: its conditional is fully regular, *sería, serías, sería...*, built straight off the infinitive like any other verb.',
